    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d365">

                <pb facs="0915" n="867" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v06p1/pg867"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">741.5 MSP/3–1152</note>
                <head>No. 365<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Prime Minister Churchill to President
                            Truman</hi><anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d365-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1952-03-10">March 10, 1952</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>You will no doubt have seen what has been happening over here and I am sure you
                    will be interested in the Budget. Our defense programme is already somewhat
                    spread out. It is not certain that even with a struggle we shall be able to
                    fulfill it. I quite understand you have your difficulties as well as we. When I
                    was over with you there was much talk of “offshore purchases” which could help
                    the N.A.T.O. front and enable us to fulfill our
                        programme.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d365-f2"/></p>
                <p>It would be possible for me to arrange for Canberra and Venom aircraft of the
                    latest types now being made by us in the United Kingdom to be delivered to the
                    United States and distributed by you to N.A.T.O. wherever Eisenhower thought they could be most useful.</p>
                <p>If as I hope, you think these ideas are worth pursuing I suggest that our people
                    should talk to Mr. Batt in London and
                    that Franks should discuss the
                    matter with Averell Harriman.</p>
                <p>Thank you for what you said about the little package of meat.</p>
                <p>A memo follows.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d365-f3"/></p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d365-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d365-f1-content">
                <p>This message was delivered to Secretary Acheson by Ambassador Franks on Mar. 11 and was attached to a memorandum by Battle to the White House, along with
                        Eden’s message to Acheson (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >infra</hi>), which asked that they be cabled to President Truman who was then in Florida.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d365-f2" n="2" xml:id="d365-f2-content">
                <p>For documentation on the Truman–Churchill
                    talks, held Jan. 5–18, at Washington, see <ref target="#d311">Documents
                        311</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d365-f3" n="3" xml:id="d365-f3-content">
                <p>Not printed; the <hi rendition="#italic">aide-mémoire</hi>, dated Mar. 12,
                    presented a detailed proposal for the United States to accept several hundred
                    jet aircraft from the United Kingdom, thus earning dollars for the British and
                    easing their balance of payments problem. (741.5 MSP/3–1252)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
